# Dual-backend crypto: keyring + universal envelope (hard requirement)

Operator directive (non-negotiable): **HSM and AWS-LC software must BOTH
always be available for every encrypt/decrypt.** Keys and engines migrate
between platforms with no interruption. Every secret carries metadata
identifying which key it needs, so rotation (decrypt-with-old,
re-wrap-with-new) works on **every** platform including HSM.

The canonical contract lives in **`pim-crypto-adapter`**
(`src/key_manager.rs`): `Keyring`, `KeyEntry`, `KeyState`, `KekCustody`,
`UniversalEnvelope`, `DualLiveKekProvider`, `SoftwareKekStore`, and
`KeyManager`. Do not fork or re-invent these shapes per crate — depend on the
adapter and map its `UniversalEnvelope` onto your storage columns/files.

## The only way to produce ciphertext

- Produce every at-rest secret through `KeyManager::encode(keyring_id, aad,
  plaintext) -> UniversalEnvelope`. It refuses to emit without full
  key-lookup metadata (`keyring_id`, `key_id`, `adapter_id`, alg, nonces,
  tag, `aad_context`).
- Decrypt only through `KeyManager::decode(&UniversalEnvelope) ->
  Zeroizing<Vec<u8>>` (or `decode_with_ciphertext` when content streams from
  a file). Decode **resolves the key from the envelope's `key_id` /
  `adapter_id`** — NEVER a global "current default" KEK.
- **Forbidden outside `pim-crypto-adapter`:** raw `aws_lc_rs::aead::*`,
  bespoke `aes_256_gcm_*` calls, the legacy `encrypt_envelope` /
  `decrypt_envelope`, and any `v{n}:aes256gcm:` string construction. An
  enforcement integration test (modelled on `forbid_env_set_var.rs`) fails
  the build if these appear in server/client/config-storage source.
- `ring` is **never** an active provider. Both backends are FIPS/CMVP:
  software = AWS-LC #4759; HSM records its own `cmvp_cert`. New writes refuse
  `cmvp_cert == "pending"` unless an explicit lab override is set.

## Keyring model

- Exactly one `KeyState::Active` key per keyring for new writes.
- `Retired` (and `Compromised`) keys stay loadable so old ciphertext decrypts
  until a re-wrap sweep leaves zero references; only then remove them.
- Software KEKs are stored **wrapped at rest** via `SoftwareKekStore`
  (DPAPI/keyring in `pim-config-storage`) and loaded transiently into a
  zeroized buffer — never plaintext `REG_SZ`, never raw bytes in a persisted
  keyring row (`KekCustody::SoftwareWrapped { custody_id }`). HSM keys are
  on-token handles (`KekCustody::Hsm { label, id_hex }`).

## Dual-live selection, rotation, migration

- Construct BOTH the PKCS#11 HSM adapter and the AWS-LC software adapter at
  startup and keep them live simultaneously in one `DualLiveKekProvider`.
- New writes use the Active key's engine; if that engine is unreachable and a
  `software_fallback_key_id` is set, write under the fallback and stamp ITS
  `key_id` (still self-describing).
- **Rotation** and **engine migration** share one primitive:
  `KeyManager::rewrap(&env)` decrypts the DEK with the envelope's *named* key
  and re-wraps it under the keyring's current Active key. The content
  ciphertext, nonce, and tag stay **byte-for-byte identical** — only the DEK
  wrap changes. Migration = stage `PendingActivation` on the new engine →
  `activate_key` → re-wrap sweep (both engines live so reads never fail) →
  remove the old key when unreferenced.

## No-panic / fail-closed

Propagate `KeyManagerError` and `tracing`; never `unwrap`/`expect`/`panic` on
a crypto path. A crypto failure is a hard failure — audit-log it and fail the
request closed. US English in all messages and identifiers.

## FIPS DLL staging

If a binary relinks against the FIPS module, stage DLLs per
`fips-dll-staging.mdc`.
